(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-06"] = {"6.1":{"s":"A mechanical wave is a disturbance that travels through an elastic medium because its particles vibrate about their mean positions. Energy and momentum travel with the wave, but the matter of the medium does not. Electromagnetic waves need no medium. This chapter covers reflection of waves, superposition, stationary waves, vibrations of strings and air columns, beats and musical sound."},"6.2":{"s":"A progressive (travelling) wave carries a disturbance steadily in one direction. Every particle repeats the same vibration with the same amplitude and frequency, but the phase changes from particle to particle. In a transverse wave the particles move at right angles to the direction of travel; in a longitudinal wave, along it. Longitudinal waves travel through solids, liquids and gases; transverse mechanical waves need a solid.","f":[{"x":"y = A*sin(k*x - omega*t)","n":"Wave travelling towards +x; k = 2 pi/lambda = wave number. For a wave towards -x use (k x + omega t).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.2, p.132"},{"x":"v = omega/k = n*lambda","n":"Wave speed; n = frequency = omega/(2 pi).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.2, p.132"}],"w":["The wave speed is fixed by the medium; the particle speed changes all the time during each vibration."]},"6.3":{"s":"At a boundary part of a wave is reflected. A transverse pulse meeting a denser medium (or a rigid end) comes back inverted: a crest returns as a trough, a phase change of π. From a rarer medium (or a free end) it returns the same way up. For a longitudinal wave, a compression reflected from a denser medium stays a compression; from a rarer medium it comes back as a rarefaction.","w":["Phase change of π on reflection only from a DENSER medium or a fixed end."]},"6.4":{"s":"Principle of superposition: when two waves cross, each produces its own displacement as if the other were absent, and the actual displacement is their vector sum. The waves pass through each other unchanged. Two waves of the same frequency with a phase difference give a wave of the same frequency whose amplitude depends on that phase difference: largest in phase, smallest in opposite phase. Noise-cancelling headphones use this.","f":[{"x":"A = sqrt(A_1^2 + A_2^2 + 2*A_1*A_2*cos(phi))","n":"Resultant amplitude of two waves of the same frequency with phase difference phi.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.4, p.136"},{"x":"I_max/I_min = (A_1 + A_2)^2/(A_1 - A_2)^2","n":"Intensity goes as amplitude squared: largest at phi = 0, least at phi = pi.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.4, p.136"}],"w":["Add the amplitudes, then square for intensity; do not add the intensities."]},"6.5":{"s":"Two identical waves travelling in opposite directions along the same line, such as a wave and its reflection on a string, combine into a stationary (standing) wave. It does not move along: nodes stay at rest, antinodes vibrate with the largest amplitude, and the amplitude changes from point to point. All particles in one loop move in phase; neighbouring loops are opposite in phase. No energy is carried along.","f":[{"x":"y = 2*a*cos(2*pi*x/lambda)*sin(omega*t)","n":"Stationary wave; a = amplitude of each travelling wave. The amplitude 2a cos(2 pi x/lambda) depends on the position x.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.5, p.138"},{"x":"x = (2*p - 1)*lambda/4","n":"Nodes (p = 1, 2, 3 ...), measured from an antinode at x = 0; antinodes at x = p lambda/2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.5, p.138"}],"w":["Node to node = λ/2; node to the next antinode = λ/4.","A stationary wave carries no energy along it; the energy stays in each loop."]},"6.6":{"s":"Free vibration: a body disturbed and left alone vibrates at its own natural frequency, and the amplitude slowly dies away. Forced vibration: an outside periodic force makes the body vibrate at the force's frequency; the closer that frequency is to the natural frequency, the larger the amplitude. When they are equal, the amplitude is largest: resonance.","w":["In forced vibration the body follows the DRIVER's frequency, not its own."]},"6.7":{"s":"A string or air column vibrates only in modes fixed by its ends. The lowest is the fundamental (first harmonic); higher ones are overtones. A pipe closed at one end has a node at the closed end and an antinode just beyond the open end (end correction), so only odd harmonics occur. A pipe open at both ends and a string fixed at both ends give all harmonics. A string's frequency depends on length, tension and mass per length.","f":[{"x":"n = v/(4*(l + e))","n":"Pipe closed at one end, fundamental; e = end correction = 0.3 d, d = inner diameter. Only odd harmonics: n, 3n, 5n ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.7, p.142"},{"x":"n = v/(2*(l + 2*e))","n":"Pipe open at both ends, fundamental. All harmonics: n, 2n, 3n ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.7, p.144"},{"x":"n = (1/(2*l))*sqrt(T/m)","n":"String fixed at both ends, fundamental; T = tension, m = mass per unit length. All harmonics.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.7, p.147"}],"w":["For the same length, an open pipe's fundamental is twice a closed pipe's.","The p-th overtone of a closed pipe is (2p + 1)n; of an open pipe (p + 1)n. Overtones are not harmonics in general."]},"6.8":{"s":"A sonometer is a hollow wooden box with a wire stretched over two movable bridges, kept taut by weights. The box amplifies the sound. The wire is set into vibration with a tuning fork; adjusting the bridges until a paper rider on the wire flies off finds resonance. It is used to check the laws of a vibrating string: frequency goes as 1/l, as √T and as 1/√m.","w":["At resonance the rider sits at an antinode, in the middle of the vibrating length."]},"6.9":{"s":"Two sound waves of the same amplitude and slightly different frequencies, heard together, rise and fall in loudness regularly: this is beats. One loud (waxing) and one soft (waning) make one beat. The number of beats per second equals the difference of the two frequencies. The ear can count beats only up to about 6-7 per second. Beats are used to tune instruments and to find an unknown frequency.","f":[{"x":"N = n_1 - n_2","n":"Beat frequency: difference of the two frequencies.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.9, p.151"},{"x":"T = 1/(n_1 - n_2)","n":"Time between two loud sounds (period of beats).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.9, p.151"}],"w":["Loading a fork with wax LOWERS its frequency; filing it raises it. Check whether the beats rise or fall."]},"6.10":{"s":"Sound has three characteristics. Loudness is how strong the sound feels; it depends on intensity and is measured on a logarithmic scale in decibels. Pitch depends on frequency: a higher frequency sounds shriller. Quality (timbre) depends on which overtones are present and how strong they are; it lets us tell two instruments apart at the same pitch and loudness.","f":[{"x":"beta = 10*log_10(I/I_0)","n":"Sound level in decibels; I_0 = 10^-12 W/m^2, the faintest audible intensity.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.10, p.153"}],"w":["Ten times the intensity adds only 10 dB."]},"6.11":{"s":"Musical instruments use stationary waves. Stringed instruments (plucked, bowed or struck: sitar, violin, santoor, piano) use vibrating strings. Wind instruments (flute, harmonium, clarinet) use vibrating air columns. Percussion instruments (tabla, drum, cymbals) use stretched membranes or metal plates. The mix of fundamental and overtones gives each instrument its own sound."}};
